{
  "slug": "artist:prince-buster",
  "name": "Prince Buster",
  "editorial_article": "Born Cecil Bustamente Campbell in Kingston, Jamaica, in 1938, Prince Buster became a central architect of ska and rocksteady music through his early production of tracks like the Folkes Brothers' 1961 hit 'Oh Carolina'. During the 1960s, he operated the 'Voice of the People' sound system and released influential singles on his own imprints, including the Blue Beat and Fab labels, featuring musicians like Ernest Ranglin and Rico Rodriguez. His UK chart presence was marked by the 1967 success of 'Al Capone', which reached number 18, and the 1998 hit 'Whine and Grine'. His output profoundly shaped the UK ska revival, with Madness deriving their name from his 1963 track 'Madness' and The Specials utilizing his compositions for their own work. In 2001, he received the Order of Distinction from the Jamaican government for his contributions to the country's musical heritage.",
